>
  <p class="description scribble-target">Every high-performing post has a lifespan. Understanding why traffic declines — and intervening before rankings slip — is one of the highest-leverage activities in content operations.</p>

  <h2 class="note-heading">What Content Decay Is</h2>
  <p class="note-text">Content decay is the gradual decline in organic traffic and rankings that affects most published content over time. It is not a penalty or an algorithm shift — it is entropy. The web adds new content daily, competitors improve their coverage, and Google's understanding of a topic deepens. A post that ranked first in 2023 faces a materially different competitive set in 2026.</p>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>Decay is normal and expected. The question is not whether it will happen, but when and how fast.</li>
    <li>High-traffic posts decay faster in competitive verticals. A ranking in fintech or health can lose 50% of its traffic within twelve months without active maintenance.</li>
    <li>Informational content decays faster than navigational content. Brand queries are stable; topic queries shift as the information landscape evolves.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">Early Warning Signals</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li><strong>Click-through rate drop</strong> — if impressions hold but clicks decline, your title and meta description are losing relevance against updated SERP features or competitors.</li>
    <li><strong>Average position creep</strong> — a gradual position decline of two to three spots over three months is a reliable early signal, often invisible in weekly reporting.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyword cannibalisation</strong> — newer internal content covering related sub-topics may be splitting ranking signals across multiple URLs.</li>
    <li><strong>Backlink profile stagnation</strong> — while competitors in the same topic attract new links, a decaying post receives none. Link velocity is a leading indicator.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">The Refresh Framework</h2>
  <ul class="note-list">
    <li>Run a content audit quarterly against GSC data. Flag any post that has lost more than 20% of its impressions or clicks quarter-over-quarter.</li>
    <li>Compare your content against the current top three results for your target keyword. Identify structural gaps — sections they cover that you don't, depth differences, recency of data cited.</li>
    <li>Update statistics, tool references, and year-specific guidance first. These are easy wins with immediate relevance signals.</li>
    <li>Re-optimise the title tag and H1 against current SERP intent. Intent shifts — a post written for informational intent may now face commercial competitors.</li>
    <li>Add new sections where competitors outperform you on depth. Expanding word count is only valuable if the added content directly addresses a gap.</li>
    <li>Signal freshness to Google by updating the <code>dateModified</code> in your schema markup and submitting the URL to IndexNow after publishing changes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="note-heading">When to Consolidate Instead of Refresh</h2>
  <p class="note-text">Not every decaying post is worth saving. If a URL has thin content covering a topic that a better-performing sibling post covers more thoroughly, consolidation via 301 redirect is often the right call. Canonical authority concentrates, the stronger post gets a signal boost, and the site's overall quality threshold rises.</p>
